  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Outside of some patchy fog/low stratus this \nmorning above normal temperatures return Today and Friday. Dry \nweather pattern is looking likely for Halloween weekend and into \nearly next week. \n\nEarly morning weather analysis shows weak zonal SW flow aloft. While \nat the surface, a 1028mb high pressure sits off the New England \ncoast. The pressure gradient over the area remains weak allowing for \nweak NE onshore flow to occur. Temperatures and dew points  are \nranging between the upper 40s to low 50s inland and upper 50s to low \n60s across SE VA and NE NC. In addition, due to clear skies, higher \ndews, and light winds across the piedmont and interior SE VA and NE \nNC, a low stratus deck and patchy fog has developed. Through the \nrest of the morning the stratus and patchy fog will continue to \nexpand and cause reduced visibilities. No SPS or Dense Fog has been \nissued at this time due to Visibilities staying up. However, if \nconditions worsen through the early morning an SPS or Dense Fog may \npotentially be issued. \n\nFor today, the stratus and patchy fog is likely to burn off by 10am \ngiving way to another nice fall day. Highs today range from the \nupper 60s across the immediate coast and low to mid-70s elsewhere. \nBy Friday, the high pressure will be pushed south due to an \napproaching cold front. This cold front is expected to push through \nthe area by Friday afternoon. However, its initial push is to advect \ndrier air as the cooler air lags behind. This will allow for \nFridays temps to make a run into the upper 70s and low 80s. These \ntemperatures mixed with low 50s dews will give way to RH of 35-40% \nin the afternoon across interior VA and NE NC. In addition, \nbreezier conditions are possible especially across the Northern Neck \nand Eastern Shore where the stronger upper level winds reside. Gusts \nupwards of 20 to 25mph cannot be ruled out. By Friday night the \ncooler air returns helping temperatures back to near/just above \nclimatological normal for the weekend. \n\nDry weather conditions are forecasted to prevail through the late \nweekend and into early, as upper ridging builds over the region. \nHalloween continues to look dry pleasant, with increasing clouds \nahead of of a weak cold front allowing for optimal Trick-or-Treating \nconditions across the region. Looking Monday into early next week \nthe potential for the pattern to become slightly unsettled continues \nto remain low due to high pressure aloft sitting over the area. The \ndeterministic models and their ensembles have completely back off on \nany rain chances during the first couple days of November. The next \nbest chance of rain may potentially come late next week. However, \nconfidence is low at this time due to how far we are out and some \nensemble disagreement.",
